= Female-Specific Reproductive Lifespan and ME/CFS
<ch:female-reproductive-axis>

ME/CFS is diagnosed roughly three to four times more often in women than in men @Thomas2022sexDifferences @Shah2025sexDifferencesLongCOVID, and the conditions most frequently comorbid with it — mast cell activation syndrome (MCAS), postural orthostatic tachycardia syndrome (POTS), and hypermobile Ehlers–Danlos syndrome (hEDS) — also cluster strongly in females. This chapter treats the female reproductive axis as a first-class determinant of disease expression: a hormonal lifespan that runs from menarche through the menstrual cycle, pregnancy, the postpartum window, perimenopause, and menopause, each stage carrying its own consequences for ME/CFS severity, onset, and management. The endocrine mechanisms that tie these stages together are introduced in Chapter @ch:endocrine and the sex-hormone-specific findings in its sex-hormone section @sec:sex-hormones; this chapter assembles those strands into a coherent lifespan narrative.

The clinical stakes are concrete. Menstrual-cycle phase modulates symptom burden in many premenopausal patients @sec:sex-hormones. Pregnancy produces a striking tripartite outcome — a substantial minority improve, a minority worsen, and many are unchanged — and the postpartum window is a recognised vulnerability for onset and relapse @Schacterle2004pregnancy @Slack2023pregnancyReview. Perimenopause and menopause are periods of symptom acceleration for some women, and early menopause is over-represented in ME/CFS @boneva2015menopause @Stewart2024menopausePostCOVID. Hormone replacement therapy (HRT) is a common clinical question, yet its benefit in ME/CFS is unproven while its safety profile is well characterised and route-dependent @Johansson2024HRTCardiovascular @Goldstajn2023transdermalOralHRT. Endometriosis co-occurs at a meta-analytic ~2.8-fold elevation, an association that is robust though partly based on self-reported cases @Compton2025endometriosis, and polycystic ovary syndrome (PCOS) is a frequently asked-about comorbidity that currently lacks direct evidence of association.

A dedicated chapter is warranted because reproductive hormones are not a side issue — they are a modifiable axis through which disease expression changes across a woman's life, an endogenous source of symptomatic variability that can be mistaken for treatment failure, and a plausible mechanistic bridge to the MCAS/POTS/hEDS cluster via estrogen–progesterone modulation of mast cell activity @Zaitsu2007estradiolMastCell @Jensen2010estradiolProgesteroneMastCell. Throughout, the evidence is graded by certainty, translation gaps between model systems and patients are flagged, and severity applicability is stated wherever the literature permits — and stated as unknown where it does not.

*Structure of the chapter.* Section @sec:fra-cyclicity examines menstrual-cycle phase effects on symptoms and their physiological basis. Section @sec:fra-pregnancy covers pregnancy outcome data, obstetric management, and the postpartum window. Section @sec:fra-menopause addresses perimenopause, menopause, and early menopause. Section @sec:fra-hrt assesses hormone replacement therapy, including the estrogen–progesterone–mast-cell interaction. Section @sec:fra-endometriosis-pcos reviews endometriosis and the open question of PCOS. Section @sec:fra-synthesis assembles the reproductive-lifespan model.

#roadmap(title: [How to Use This Chapter])[
*For patients*: read the menstrual-cyclicity and perimenopause/menopause sections to understand cycle- and life-stage-linked symptom flares, and the HRT section if hormone therapy is under consideration.

*For caregivers*: read the menstrual-cyclicity and pregnancy/postpartum sections to understand symptom variability and the postpartum vulnerability window. Read the structure-of-chapter mapping to navigate sections.

*For clinicians*: read the pregnancy, HRT, and endometriosis/PCOS sections for management decisions, noting that HRT benefit in ME/CFS is unproven and route-dependent. The mast-cell axis is central.

*For researchers*: read the synthesis (reproductive-lifespan model) and the sex-hormone interactions; these cross-link to Chapter @ch:endocrine and the female-predominance question.
]

